- How would you extend to a sliding-window setting that only counts the last W elements?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about incremental data structures and Bloom filter trade-offs for query pruning in streaming text.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental suffix automaton with amortized O(1) update per character
- Bloom filters for O(1) query pruning per pattern, per batch
- Cache behavior: larger B reduces SAM update frequency but increases Bloom filter staleness and memory pressure
- Space: each block's Bloom filter costs O(B) bits; false positives introduce extra SAM verifications

## Code Example
```javascript
// Extend SAM with a block of text
function extendSAM(sam, block) {
  for (const char of block) {
    sam.extend(char);
  }
}

// Build Bloom filter for a block's substrings
function buildBlockBloom(block, size) {
  const bloom = new BloomFilter(size, 0.01);
  for (let i = 0; i < block.length; i++) {
    for (let len = 1; len <= block.length - i; len++) {
      bloom.add(block.slice(i, i + len));
    }
  }
  return bloom;
}

// Query patterns against accumulated text
function queryPatterns(sam, bloomFilters, patterns) {
  return patterns.map(p => {
    const mightMatch = bloomFilters.every(bf => bf.test(p));
    return mightMatch && sam.contains(p);
  });
}
```

## Why This Is Asked\n\nTests understanding of streaming frequency estimation under tight memory. The MG approach gives a bounded-memory alternative to exact counts, with concrete guarantees and practical trade-offs in real-time systems.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- Streaming algorithms, Misra-Gries, bounded counters, frequency estimation, error bounds, exact vs. approximate modes.\n- Trade-offs: k vs accuracy, batch size B vs throughput.\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
class MGMode {
  constructor(k){
    this.k = k;
    this.counters = new Map();
  }
  add(x){
    if (this.counters.has(x)) this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x)+1);
    else if (this.counters.size < this.k) this.counters.set(x,1);
    else {
      for (const key of Array.from(this.counters.keys()))
        this.counters.set(key, this.counters.get(key)-1);
      for (const [key,c] of Array.from(this.counters))
        if (c<=0) this.counters.delete(key);
    }
  }
  estimate(){
    let best = null, bestc = -1;
    for (const [k,c] of this.counters) if (c>bestc){ bestc=c; best=k; }
    return best;
  }
}
```

